## The requirements you're identifying

You're right that there are requirements for these tools to actually work for neutered agents. Based on what the repo already demonstrates, here's the list:

**1. The tool must fetch context the AI would otherwise burn tokens reading.**
`onboard_agent.py` does this — it reads `STANDARDS_REGISTRY.json`, `AGENT_FEED.md`, `consent_log.jsonl`, runs `generate_feed_entry.py --verify`, and outputs a single pre-digested context block. The AI reads one output instead of 8 files. [4-cite-11](#4-cite-11) 

**2. The tool must enumerate what exists so the AI doesn't have to discover it.**
`scope_audit.py` enumerates all domains, all files per domain, all functions per file. `tautology_detector.py` enumerates every `check_*` function and classifies it. The AI gets a JSON report — it doesn't need to `ls` and `cat` 254 directories. [4-cite-12](#4-cite-12) 

**3. The tool must be deterministic and run without AI involvement.**
Every tool in the 8a suite uses `Fraction` arithmetic (no floats), returns `Tuple[bool, ProofObject]`, and runs with `python3 <script>` from repo root using only stdlib. No LLM calls. No API keys. No tokens burned. [4-cite-13](#4-cite-13) 

**4. The tool must know the agent's budget.**
`context_window_estimator.py` knows Kimi has 220k tokens, Copilot has 128k, Claude has 200k. It tells the agent what it can afford to read before it reads it. [4-cite-14](#4-cite-14) 

**5. The tool must detect what the AI got wrong without the AI needing to self-audit.**
`scope_reduction_detector.py` compares campaign spec (what was asked) against filesystem (what exists). If the AI built 30% of what was requested, the tool catches it — the AI doesn't need to spend tokens auditing itself. [4-cite-15](#4-cite-15) 

**6. The tool must give the AI a decision, not raw data.**
The `verify_all.py` wrapper doesn't dump 10 raw outputs — it prints a summary table with PASS/FAIL/INFO per check. The AI reads one table and knows exactly what to fix. That's the "smarter than the AI" part — within the verification domain, the tool already made the judgment. [4-cite-16](#4-cite-16) 

